WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
A cell must give each daughter one copy of every chromosome.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.860
Each chromosome is two sister copies, held together by a ring called cohesin.

3
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.880
Each sister has a kinetochore, the machine that grips the spindle fibres.

4
00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.940
Fibres from opposite poles pull on the sisters.

5
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.080
So what lets go, and why can the cell never take it back?

6
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:34.013
First the cell checks its attachments.

7
00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:37.453
The right outcome is biorientation.

8
00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:41.353
The two sister kinetochores attach to opposite poles.

9
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:46.753
That puts the sisters under tension, because the poles pull against the cohesin.

10
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:49.993
Tension stabilizes the right attachments.

11
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:54.173
A kinetochore that is still unattached sends a signal.

12
00:00:54.713 --> 00:01:01.000
That signal holds back the anaphase-promoting complex, with its coactivator, Cdc20.

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.753
When every kinetochore is attached, the signal stops.

14
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:09.227
Now the anaphase-promoting complex is released.

15
00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:13.867
It tags proteins with ubiquitin, which sends them to the proteasome.

16
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:16.287
One target is securin.

17
00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:21.227
Securin binds an enzyme called separase and keeps it inactive.

18
00:01:22.027 --> 00:01:28.027
The tagged securin is destroyed, and in vertebrates so is cyclin B, which lifts a

19
00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:31.327
second brake. Nothing holds separase back now.

20
00:01:31.327 --> 00:01:33.087
Separase is free.

21
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.140
Separase is a protease.

22
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.640
It cuts Scc1, one subunit of the cohesin ring, and the ring opens.

23
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.120
A cut protein cannot be rejoined.

24
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.120
So the sisters are free to part.

25
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:56.000
In budding yeast cells held at metaphase, cutting Scc1 was enough to separate the sisters.

26
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.430
Anaphase has two movements.

27
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:04.050
Anaphase A moves each chromosome toward its pole, as the fibres shorten.

28
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.890
Anaphase B separates the poles themselves.

29
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.210
Take an illustrative movie.

30
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:15.010
The poles start 12.0 μm apart and end 16.0 μm apart.

31
00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:22.430
Each kinetochore moves from 5.5 to 1.5 μm from its pole, in 4.0 minutes.

32
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.490
The sisters separate at 3.0 μm per minute.

33
00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.210
That is twice anaphase A, plus anaphase B.

34
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.790
So anaphase is a chain with one way through.

35
00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.170
Every kinetochore is attached.

36
00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.090
Securin is destroyed.

37
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.950
Separase cuts Scc1.

38
00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.430
Then the chromosomes and the poles move apart.

39
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:52.810
Keep this rule. A cut protein cannot be rejoined, so anaphase cannot be reversed.
